              "output": "[{\"cellType\":\"markdown\",\"source\":\"# Exercise: Doctor Who on holidays\\n\\n*Doctor Who* is a British TV show. Since 2005, the BBC has shown a special episode almost every **Christmas Day**. Some years, it was on **New Year's Day**.\\n\\nDo holiday episodes get more viewers? Today you use multiple regression to find out.\",\"execution_count\":null,\"cell_id\":\"d5cb08e4\"},{\"cellType\":\"markdown\",\"source\":\"## Setup\",\"execution_count\":null,\"cell_id\":\"66122a02\"},{\"cellType\":\"code\",\"source\":\"library('tidymodels')\\n\\noptions(repr.plot.res = 250, repr.plot.height = 3, repr.plot.width = 5)\",\"execution_count\":1,\"cell_id\":\"13e3ae6a\",\"language\":\"R\",\"outputs\":[{\"output_type\":\"stream\",\"text\":\"\u2500\u2500 \\u001b[1mAttaching packages\\u001b[22m \u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500 tidymodels 1.4.1 \u2500\u2500\\n\\u001b[32m\u2714\\u001b[39m \\u001b[34mbroom       \\u001b[39m 1.0.13     \\u001b[32m\u2714\\u001b[39m \\u001b[34mrecipes     \\u001b[39m 1.4.0 \\n\\u001b[32m\u2714\\u001b[39m \\u001b[34mdials       \\u001b[39m 1.4.4      \\u001b[32m\u2714\\u001b[39m \\u001b[34mrsample     \\u001b[39m 1.3.2 \\n\\u001b[32m\u2714\\u001b[39m \\u001b[34mdplyr       \\u001b[39m 1.2.1      \\u001b[32m\u2714\\u001b[39m \\u001b[34mtailor      \\u001b[39m 0.1.0 \\n\\u001b[32m\u2714\\u001b[39m \\u001b[34mggplot2     \\u001b[39m 4.0.3      \\u001b[32m\u2714\\u001b[39m \\u001b[34mtidyr       \\u001b[39m 1.3.2 \\n\\u001b[32m\u2714\\u001b[39m \\u001b[34minfer       \\u001b[39m 1.1.0      \\u001b[32m\u2714\\u001b[39m \\u001b[34mtune        \\u001b[39m 2.1.0 \\n\\u001b[32m\u2714\\u001b[39m \\u001b[34mmodeldata   \\u001b[39m 1.6.0      \\u001b[32m\u2714\\u001b[39m \\u001b[34mworkflows   \\u001b[39m 1.3.0 \\n\\u001b[32m\u2714\\u001b[39m \\u001b[34mparsnip     \\u001b[39m 1.6.1      \\u001b[32m\u2714\\u001b[39m \\u001b[34mworkflowsets\\u001b[39m 1.1.1 \\n\\u001b[32m\u2714\\u001b[39m \\u001b[34mpurrr       \\u001b[39m 1.2.2      \\u001b[32m\u2714\\u001b[39m \\u001b[34myardstick   \\u001b[39m 1.4.0 \\n\u2500\u2500 \\u001b[1mConflicts\\u001b[22m \u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500 tidymodels_conflicts() \u2500\u2500\\n\\u001b[31m\u2716\\u001b[39m \\u001b[34mtidyr\\u001b[39m::\\u001b[32mcomplete()\\u001b[39m masks \\u001b[34mhera\\u001b[39m::complete()\\n\\u001b[31m\u2716\\u001b[39m \\u001b[34mpurrr\\u001b[39m::\\u001b[32mdiscard()\\u001b[39m  masks \\u001b[34mscales\\u001b[39m::discard()\\n\\u001b[31m\u2716\\u001b[39m \\u001b[34mdplyr\\u001b[39m::\\u001b[32mfilter()\\u001b[39m   masks \\u001b[34mstats\\u001b[39m::filter()\\n\\u001b[31m\u2716\\u001b[39m \\u001b[34mdplyr\\u001b[39m::\\u001b[32mlag()\\u001b[39m      masks \\u001b[34mstats\\u001b[39m::lag()\\n\\u001b[31m\u2716\\u001b[39m \\u001b[34mrecipes\\u001b[39m::\\u001b[32mstep()\\u001b[39m   masks \\u001b[34mstats\\u001b[39m::step()\\n\\u001b[34m\u2022\\u001b[39m Use suppressPackageStartupMessages() to eliminate package startup messages\\n\"}]},{\"cellType\":\"markdown\",\"source\":\"## The data\\n\\nEach row is one episode, from 2005 to 2022.\\n\\n| column | meaning |\\n|---|---|\\n| `episode_title` | name of the episode |\\n| `type` | `episode` or `special` |\\n| `first_aired` | date the episode was first on TV |\\n| `uk_viewers` | UK viewers, in millions |\\n| `rating` | audience score, 0\u2013100 |\\n\\nSource: [TidyTuesday, 2023-11-28](https://github.com/rfordatascience/tidytuesday/tree/main/data/2023/2023-11-28) (from Wikipedia, via the `datardis` package).\\n\\nWe make two new columns:\\n\\n- `holiday`: 1 if the episode aired on December 25 or January 1, 0 if not.\\n- `year`: the air date as a number, like 2010.5 for the middle of 2010. `decimal_date()` does this.\",\"execution_count\":null,\"cell_id\":\"b4039b68\"},{\"cellType\":\"code\",\"source\":\"drwho <- read.csv('https://raw.githubusercontent.com/rfordatascience/tidytuesday/main/data/2023/2023-11-28/drwho_episodes.csv')\\n\\ndrwho <- drwho |>\\n    mutate(\\n        first_aired = as.Date(first_aired),\\n        holiday = if_else(format(first_aired, '%m-%d') %in% c('12-25', '01-01'), 1, 0),\\n        year = lubridate::decimal_date(first_aired)\\n    )\\n\\ndrwho |> count(holiday)\",\"execution_count\":2,\"cell_id\":\"0b936f0f\",\"language\":\"R\",\"outputs\":[{\"output_type\":\"execute_result\",\"text\":\"  holiday n  \\n1 0       157\\n2 1        18\",\"image\":null}]},{\"cellType\":\"markdown\",\"source\":\"## 1. Look at the data\\n\\nPlot `uk_viewers` (y-axis) against `first_aired` (x-axis). Color the points by `factor(holiday)`.\\n\\n<details>\\n<summary>Hint</summary>\\n\\n```R\\nggplot(drwho, aes(x = first_aired, y = uk_viewers, color = factor(holiday))) +\\n    geom_point()\\n```\\n\\n</details>\",\"execution_count\":null,\"cell_id\":\"b9802e90\"},{\"cellType\":\"code\",\"source\":\"ggplot(drwho, aes(x = first_aired, y = uk_viewers, color = factor(holiday))) +\\n    geom_point()\",\"execution_count\":3,\"cell_id\":\"917707be\",\"language\":\"R\",\"outputs\":[{\"output_type\":\"stream\",\"text\":\"Outputs are too large to include. Use command with: cat <notebook_path> | jq '.cells[6].outputs'\"}]},{\"cellType\":\"markdown\",\"source\":\"What happens to the number of viewers over time? Where are the holiday points?\",\"execution_count\":null,\"cell_id\":\"5157ec89\"},{\"cellType\":\"markdown\",\"source\":\"## 2. One predictor\\n\\nFit `uk_viewers ~ holiday` with `linear_reg()`. Save it as `fit_one` and show `tidy(fit_one)`.\\n\\n<details>\\n<summary>Hint</summary>\\n\\n```R\\nfit_one <- linear_reg() |> fit(uk_viewers ~ holiday, data = drwho)\\n\\nfit_one\\n```\\n\\n</details>\",\"execution_count\":null,\"cell_id\":\"9b78c04a\"},{\"cellType\":\"code\",\"source\":\"fit_one <- linear_reg() |> fit(uk_viewers ~ holiday, data = drwho)\\n\\ntidy(fit_one)\",\"execution_count\":4,\"cell_id\":\"ef8eba46\",\"language\":\"R\",\"outputs\":[{\"output_type\":\"execute_result\",\"text\":\"  term        estimate std.error statistic p.value      \\n1 (Intercept) 7.161083 0.1254197 57.096939 3.531246e-114\\n2 holiday     2.300028 0.3910647  5.881452  2.051807e-08\",\"image\":null}]},{\"cellType\":\"markdown\",\"source\":\"**Check:** the `holiday` estimate should be about 2.3. What does this number mean, in words?\",\"execution_count\":null,\"cell_id\":\"05309f57\"},{\"cellType\":\"markdown\",\"source\":\"## 3. AI step: add `year`\\n\\nViewers go down over the years. We want to measure the marginal effect of `holiday` on viewership with a model that also factors in `year`.\\n\\nOpen a terminal and run `opencode`.\",\"execution_count\":null,\"cell_id\":\"6d9db705\"},{\"cellType\":\"markdown\",\"source\":\"**Copy this prompt into opencode:**\",\"execution_count\":null,\"cell_id\":\"5acd831a\"},{\"cellType\":\"markdown\",\"source\":\"```text\\nI am working in exercises/12-drwho-exercise.ipynb, with R and tidymodels.\\nThe data frame drwho has a column uk_viewers (millions of viewers), a column\\nholiday (1 or 0), and a column year (the air date as a decimal year).\\n\\nIn the empty code cell in step 3, fit a linear regression of\\nuk_viewers ~ holiday + year with linear_reg(). Save it as fit_two and show\\ntidy(fit_two). Run the cell. Use tidymodels only. Do not change any other cells.\\n```\",\"execution_count\":null,\"cell_id\":\"be3bf219\"},{\"cellType\":\"markdown\",\"source\":\"**Your assistant puts its code in the cell below and runs it.** Read the code. Does the formula have both `holiday` and `year`?\",\"execution_count\":null,\"cell_id\":\"7b505814\"},{\"cellType\":\"code\",\"source\":\"fit_two <- linear_reg() |> fit(uk_viewers ~ holiday + year, data = drwho)\\n\\ntidy(fit_two)\",\"execution_count\":5,\"cell_id\":\"68fee25b\",\"language\":\"R\",\"outputs\":[{\"output_type\":\"execute_result\",\"text\":\"  term        estimate    std.error   statistic p.value     \\n1 (Intercept) 379.4484005 38.72404194  9.798781 2.727092e-18\\n2 holiday       2.5295116  0.31721374  7.974155 2.050381e-13\\n3 year         -0.1849853  0.01924147 -9.613888 8.799150e-18\",\"image\":null}]},{\"cellType\":\"markdown\",\"source\":\"**Check:** the `holiday` estimate should be about 2.5. The `year` estimate should be about -0.19.\\n\\n- What does the `year` estimate mean, in words?\\n- The `holiday` estimate changed from 2.3 to 2.5. Why? (Think about what \\\"when the other predictor stays the same\\\" means.)\",\"execution_count\":null,\"cell_id\":\"9b03c006\"},{\"cellType\":\"markdown\",\"source\":\"## 4. Plot the model\\n\\nMake a grid with `crossing(year = 2005:2023, holiday = c(0, 1))`. Predict on the grid with `fit_two` and bind the grid to the predictions.\\n\\nPlot the data with `year` on the x-axis. Add the predictions as lines, colored by `factor(holiday)`.\\n\\n<details>\\n<summary>Hint</summary>\\n\\n```R\\ngrid <- crossing(year = 2005:2023, holiday = c(0, 1))\\n\\npreds <- fit_two |>\\n    predict(new_data = grid) |>\\n    bind_cols(grid)\\n\\nggplot(drwho, aes(x = year, y = uk_viewers, color = factor(holiday))) +\\n    geom_point() +\\n    geom_line(data = preds, aes(y = .pred))\\n```\\n\\n</details>\",\"execution_count\":null,\"cell_id\":\"05249bef\"},{\"cellType\":\"code\",\"source\":\"grid <- crossing(year = 2005:2023, holiday = c(0, 1))\\n\\npreds <- fit_two |>\\n    predict(new_data = grid) |>\\n    bind_cols(grid)\\n\\nggplot(drwho, aes(x = year, y = uk_viewers, color = factor(holiday))) +\\n    geom_point() +\\n    geom_line(data = preds, aes(y = .pred))\",\"execution_count\":6,\"cell_id\":\"4eb5fb2c\",\"language\":\"R\",\"outputs\":[{\"output_type\":\"stream\",\"text\":\"Outputs are too large to include. Use command with: cat <notebook_path> | jq '.cells[18].outputs'\"}]},{\"cellType\":\"markdown\",\"source\":\"**Check:** you should see two parallel lines that go down. The holiday line is about 2.5 million viewers higher.\",\"execution_count\":null,\"cell_id\":\"895c2f2c\"},{\"cellType\":\"markdown\",\"source\":\"## 5. Bonus: better or just bigger?\\n\\nMore people watch on holidays. But do they *like* the holiday episodes more?\\n\\nFit `rating ~ holiday + year`. Look at the `holiday` estimate.\",\"execution_count\":null,\"cell_id\":\"31fc556e\"},{\"cellType\":\"code\",\"source\":\"fit_rating <- linear_reg() |> fit(rating ~ holiday + year, data = drwho)\\n\\ntidy(fit_rating)\",\"execution_count\":7,\"cell_id\":\"d9b2fd04\",\"language\":\"R\",\"outputs\":[{\"output_type\":\"execute_result\",\"text\":\"  term        estimate    std.error   statistic  p.value     \\n1 (Intercept) 854.2207048 82.84323597 10.3112909 1.029956e-19\\n2 holiday      -0.3030026  0.67862267 -0.4464965 6.558003e-01\\n3 year         -0.3827139  0.04116372 -9.2973595 6.440392e-17\",\"image\":null}]},{\"cellType\":\"markdown\",\"source\":\"**Check:** the `holiday` estimate should be close to 0 (about -0.3). Holiday episodes get more viewers, but not better scores.\",\"execution_count\":null,\"cell_id\":\"0de43cfb\"}]",
